ns/spec.md`\n\n**Note**: This template is filled in by the `$speckit-plan` command; its definition describes the execution workflow.\n\n## Summary\n\nAdd `tinydb.journal.BatchWriter(db)` wrapping a supplied `TinyDB` instance\ndefensively (no changes to ordinary APIs). `apply(operations)` validates a list\nof `{'op':'insert','document':dict}` / `{'op':'remove','doc_id':int>0}`\noperations, applies them sequentially to the **default table only**, returns the\ninserted IDs (removes add nothing; empty input -> `[]`), and is fully atomic:\non any error (including a remove of a missing ID) the complete pre-batch\ndefault-table data is restored and `ValueError` propagates. Stored documents are\ndeep copies, so caller mutations after `apply` cannot affect the database, and\npreviously acquired `db.table('_default')` handles observe both success and\nrollback. Technical approach (research.md D1): one read-modify-write per batch\nagainst the table's storage instead of per-op `Table` calls.\n\n## Technical Context\n\n**Language/Version**: Python 3 (repository already uses `list[int]` annotations; 3.9+ assumed)\n\n**Primary Dependencies**: stdlib only (`copy.deepcopy`, `collections.abc.Mapping`); the existing `tinydb` package itself\n\n**Storage**: whatever `Storage` the supplied `TinyDB` instance uses (e.g. `MemoryStorage` in tests); the writer uses `storage.read()`/`storage.write()` only\n\n**Testing**: pytest; public acceptance in `acceptance_public.py` (unmodified); own tests in new `tests/test_journal.py`\n\n**Target Platform**: any platform running the Python interpreter (library)\n\n**Project Type**: library\n\n**Performance Goals**: N/A for this milestone (in-memory correctness feature); one storage read and at most one write per `apply` call\n\n**Constraints**: no changes to `tinydb/table.py`, `tinydb/database.py`, `tinydb/storages.py` public behavior; default table only; `ValueError` is the only error type for invalid input\n\n"}